{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 16, :comments ["<b>Behold, you are lying down with your fathers, and the nation will arise, etc.</b> Our Rabbis, of blessed memory (Sanhedrin 90b), learned from here a scriptural support for the resurrection of the dead from the Torah, as it says: 'הִנְּךָ שׁוֹכֵב עִם אֲבוֹתֶיךָ וְקָם.' And this derivation is very astonishing, for who does not know that in all these verses, the statement 'וְקָם' is attached to 'הָעָם וְזָנָה' — 'the nation, and it will go astray.' And it appears to me that they derived it from the superfluity of the word 'וְקָם,' for it should have said 'וְזָנָה הָעָם' — what is the meaning of 'וְקָם'? Rather, this is its meaning: the expression 'וְקָם' is from the language of 'כַּאֲשֶׁר יָקוּם אִישׁ עַל רֵעֵהוּ וּרְצָחוּ נֶפֶשׁ' (Deuteronomy 22:26) — 'when a man rises up against his fellow.' And just as every disciple who cites a halakhah in the name of his master — his master's lips murmur in the grave (see Yevamot 97a) — so too, when a disciple burns his master's dish in public, his master has no rest in the grave, for he should have warned his disciple, warning after warning, so that he would not come to sin, and if he did not teach him as he should have, they will say: woe to the master of this one, for thus he taught him. And in this it is as though the sinning disciple rises up against his master as an enemy and a lurker, and deprives him of his rest in the grave. And this is what is said: 'הִנְּךָ שׁוֹכֵב עִם אֲבוֹתֶיךָ' — you lie with your fathers in tranquility and in security in the grave, no adversary and no evil mishap — and suddenly lurkers will rise against you, an assembly of corrupters, for through the corruption of their deeds they rise up against you. Therefore it says: 'וְקָם הָעָם הַזֶּה וְזָנָה' etc., for through their going astray after idolatry they rise up against you. And if so, from here is a proof for the resurrection of the dead from the Torah: for if the dead will not live, if so the body in the grave is like a silent stone, neither ailing nor feeling, and whatever will be after his death will be — so how could it say that through their going astray after idolatry they will rise up against him? Rather, surely there is resurrection of the dead, and the body of the righteous still lies in its grave not severed entirely, and it knows and feels all that is done — therefore he must fear lest they rise up against him after they go astray after idolatry. Therefore you must go back and warn them, warning after warning. And this interpretation is compelling both from the side of the superfluity of the expression 'וְקָם' and from the side of His saying 'הִנְּךָ שׁוֹכֵב עִם אֲבוֹתֶיךָ,' for it should have said 'הִנְּךָ מֵת' — except that He wanted to warn him that he would lie with his fathers in tranquility, through his warning them yet again concerning idolatry."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 31, :notes {0 "The Kli Yakar cites Deuteronomy 22:26 for the phrase כַּאֲשֶׁר יָקוּם אִישׁ עַל רֵעֵהוּ; in the printed Hebrew the citation reads דברים כ״ג כו, but the verse is at 22:26 in standard enumeration. Rendered as the author intended."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:15:35.278277612Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}